Use typical content at each size boundary, then concentrate the expensive content states into two compact cases. This applies the supplied responsive-layout skill without a Cartesian explosion.

| Case | Width | Text scale | Content/state |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| Compact | 320 | 1 | Typical |
| Below breakpoint | breakpoint − 1 | 1 | Typical |
| Above breakpoint | breakpoint + 1 | 1 | Typical |
| Wide | 1280 | 1 | Typical |
| Compact accessibility | 320 | 2 | Long localized content, RTL, validation errors |
| Compact keyboard | 320 | 2 | Long localized content, RTL, validation errors, keyboard |

Use the form’s actual content breakpoint. If it has multiple breakpoints, add a boundary pair for each.

The following registration helper uses the confirmed SDK APIs. Connect `buildFixture` to your existing deterministic fixture builder and `verifyActionResult` to its observable submission or validation result.

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart';

class ProfileCase {
  const ProfileCase(
    this.name,
    this.size, {
    this.textScale = 1,
    this.longLocalizedContent = false,
    this.rtl = false,
    this.validationErrors = false,
    this.keyboardInset = 0,
  });

  final String name;
  final Size size;
  final double textScale;
  final bool longLocalizedContent;
  final bool rtl;
  final bool validationErrors;
  final double keyboardInset;
}

// Attach these keys to actual rendered components in the fixture/form.
const profileScrollKey = ValueKey('profile-scroll');
const lastFieldKey = ValueKey('profile-last-field');
const primaryActionKey = ValueKey('profile-primary-action');
const validationErrorKey = ValueKey('profile-validation-error');

void registerProfileLayoutTests({
  required double breakpoint,
  required Widget Function(ProfileCase scenario) buildFixture,
  required Future<void> Function(
    WidgetTester tester,
    ProfileCase scenario,
  ) verifyActionResult,
}) {
  final cases = <ProfileCase>[
    const ProfileCase('compact', Size(320, 640)),
    ProfileCase('below breakpoint', Size(breakpoint - 1, 800)),
    ProfileCase('above breakpoint', Size(breakpoint + 1, 800)),
    const ProfileCase('wide', Size(1280, 900)),
    const ProfileCase(
      'compact: large text, long RTL content, errors',
      Size(320, 640),
      textScale: 2,
      longLocalizedContent: true,
      rtl: true,
      validationErrors: true,
    ),
    const ProfileCase(
      'compact: large text, long RTL content, errors, keyboard',
      Size(320, 640),
      textScale: 2,
      longLocalizedContent: true,
      rtl: true,
      validationErrors: true,
      keyboardInset: 280,
    ),
  ];

  void expectContained(Rect outer, Rect inner) {
    const tolerance = 0.5;
    expect(inner.left, greaterThanOrEqualTo(outer.left - tolerance));
    expect(inner.top, greaterThanOrEqualTo(outer.top - tolerance));
    expect(inner.right, lessThanOrEqualTo(outer.right + tolerance));
    expect(inner.bottom, lessThanOrEqualTo(outer.bottom + tolerance));
  }

  for (final scenario in cases) {
    testWidgets('profile / ${scenario.name}', (tester) async {
      final view = tester.view;
      addTearDown(view.reset);

      // DPR 1 keeps physical and logical test dimensions identical.
      view.devicePixelRatio = 1;
      view.physicalSize = scenario.size;
      view.viewInsets = FakeViewPadding(
        bottom: scenario.keyboardInset,
      );

      await tester.pumpWidget(
        MaterialApp(
          builder: (context, child) => MediaQuery(
            data: MediaQuery.of(context).copyWith(
              textScaler: TextScaler.linear(scenario.textScale),
            ),
            child: Directionality(
              textDirection:
                  scenario.rtl ? TextDirection.rtl : TextDirection.ltr,
              child: child!,
            ),
          ),
          home: buildFixture(scenario),
        ),
      );
      await tester.pumpAndSettle();

      expect(tester.takeException(), isNull);

      final visibleRegion = Rect.fromLTWH(
        0,
        0,
        scenario.size.width,
        scenario.size.height - scenario.keyboardInset,
      );
      final scrollable = find.descendant(
        of: find.byKey(profileScrollKey),
        matching: find.byType(Scrollable),
      ).first;

      Future<void> reveal(Finder target) async {
        await tester.scrollUntilVisible(
          target,
          100,
          scrollable: scrollable,
        );
        await tester.ensureVisible(target);
        await tester.pumpAndSettle();
        expect(tester.takeException(), isNull);
        expectContained(visibleRegion, tester.getRect(target));
      }

      if (scenario.validationErrors) {
        await reveal(find.byKey(validationErrorKey));
      }

      final lastField = find.byKey(lastFieldKey);
      final action = find.byKey(primaryActionKey);

      await reveal(lastField);

      // Product contract: the primary action must not cover the last field.
      // Parent/child and decorative intersections are intentionally ignored.
      expect(
        tester.getRect(lastField).overlaps(tester.getRect(action)),
        isFalse,
        reason: 'The primary action must not cover the final field.',
      );

      await reveal(action);
      expect(action.hitTestable(), findsOneWidget);
      await tester.tap(action);
      await tester.pumpAndSettle();

      await verifyActionResult(tester, scenario);
      expect(tester.takeException(), isNull);
    });
  }
}
```

Keep fixture data explicit: fixed translated labels and error messages, seeded validation state, and a fake submission dependency. `Directionality` exercises RTL layout; the fixture must also supply the intended locale and localization delegates when testing actual translations.

The geometry contract assumes the last field and primary action must be disjoint. For a sticky action, also assert that the revealed field lies above the action with the required gap. Add a layout-specific assertion at the boundary cases—for example, whether the form uses one or two columns.

These tests exercise widget geometry, scrolling, and taps with a simulated keyboard inset. They do not exercise the system keyboard itself. The code has not been run against your repository.
